issue.body:
### Summary of a problem or a feature request

On this sample: https://phpstan.org/r/f6a03ce75cf484a0ffa5c0e0a1fc9a23

The value type returned by the function will be a `\DateTime` instance or `null`, nothing else.

In this case, a strict comparison like `null === getUpdatedAt()` or ``null !== getUpdatedAt()` is useless. `!getUpdatedAt()` is enough.

The reverse (dangerous weak comparison) can also be done, but it's maybe already the case on the phpstan-strict-rules extension. :thinking:

### Code snippet that reproduces the problem

```php
<?php declare(strict_type=1);

function getUpdatedAt(): ?\DateTime
{
	return 0 === rand(1, 5) % 5 ? new \DateTime('now') : null;
}

function getCount(): ?int
{
	return 0 === rand(1, 5) % 5 ? 42 : null;
}

if (null !== getUpdatedAt()) {
	echo 'test';
}

if (!getCount()) {
	echo 'test';
}
```

### Expected output

- Line `null !== getUpdatedAt()`: Useless strict comparison to null of a result that can be only a \DateTime or null
- Line `!getCount()`: Dangerous non-strict comparison of a result that can match null or 0
